: a front-run bot that can potentially help generate profits
we continue our series on open-source GitHub repositories, with the goal of eventually building something of our own
Trum3it/polymarket-frontrun-bot is an experimental trading bot for Polymarket
it is designed to monitor pending transactions on the Polygon network and react to them in real time
the bot connects to a Polygon RPC with mempool support and:
/ tracks pending transactions;
/ filters them by specific addresses or conditions;
/ attempts to submit its own order with a higher gas priority to be included in a block earlier.
architecturally, the repository looks like a long-running Node.js service that:
1/ continuously listens to the mempool;
2/ analyzes relevant transactions;
3/ makes entry decisions;
4/ submits orders to Polymarket;
5/ logs and monitors the bot’s state.
unfortunately, it’s not clear who created this bot, but it still looks interesting.
i don’t think this bot is suitable for me to implement personally, but the repository might be useful for others.
we keep digging through github repos and whether they can actually help you build a bot that makes money
user @defiance_cr built a bot and open-sourced it - so let’s break down what it is
warproxxx/poly-maker isn’t an “agent framework” - it’s a ready-to-run market making bot for Polymarket
Poly-Maker automates liquidity provision on Polymarket:
> keeps orders on both sides (YES/NO) with configurable parameters;
> watches the order book via websockets;
market management;
> adjusts spread/price based on market conditions.
in my opinion it’s better than most bots mainly because configuration is done via Google Sheets, so you don’t have to edit code every time you change something.
bot structure:
> poly_data - core market-making logic/data;
> poly_merger - position merging (based on Polymarket open-source code);
> poly_stats - account stats tracking;
> poly_utils - shared utilities;
> data_updater - separate module for collecting market info.
the main thing you must keep in mind: you’re plugging in a real private key
so if you’re going to run it, test with small balances first
i’m planning to use it as a base for my own future fork, so i can be confident everything is solid on the security side
but if you’re looking for a real foundation to start from - this is a strong option 
